+# The leechlean addressing on real data: the Leech membership predicate
+
+*Created 11 November 2026 (the intake of `source_material/ToDo/leechlean`,
+directive D22; a standalone check, written for the absorption ahead of the
+owner's build).*
+
+The leechlean item gives every recurring part of this repository's Lean
+development an **address** in the Leech lattice Λ₂₄: a 24-integer vector,
+in the ×√8 integer model, that is a true lattice point.  The codebook
+(39,992 parts) is the item's own data, built from the 442-file corpus; the
+item's Python battery re-checks all 39,992 addresses against the substrate's
+exact membership predicate (`substrate/leech2.py`) and re-runs the codec
+byte-exactly over every file of `src/lean`.  What Lean can pin down here,
+without Mathlib, is the predicate itself and the addressing on a fixed slice
+of the real data — decided, not sampled.
+
+The Conway–Sloane membership predicate (`inLeech` below, over the parity
+block `B` of `Golay/Code.lean` carried by `LeechLeanGolay.lean`): a 24-integer
+vector is in Λ iff all coordinates share one parity `m`, the positions
+congruent to 2 (mod 4) form a Golay codeword, the positions congruent to 3
+(mod 4) form a Golay codeword, and the coordinate sum is 4m (mod 8).
+
+What is proved, all by `decide` on the literal data (`slice`, `ticks`,
+`expected` are the item's own codebook entries and the first 100 ticks of
+the bundled sample stream — the file prefix of `Golay/Code.lean`):
+
+* `slice_in_leech` — every address of the slice is a true Leech lattice
+  point;
+* `slice_injective` — the slice's addresses are pairwise distinct (the
+  codebook is injective: an address names exactly one part);
+* `ticks_in_slice` — every tick of the sample stream is one of the slice's
+  addresses;
+* `roundtrip` — the address stream decodes through the codebook slice back
+  to the exact original file prefix, byte for byte;
+* `flip_breaks` — a single ±1 flip on one coordinate of a real address breaks
+  the parity congruence (the sieve's first catch);
+* `glue_stays` and `minvec_stays` — slipping by the glue vector (-3, 1²³)
+  or by a type-1 minimal vector (4, 4, 0²²) stays on the lattice: on-lattice
+  slips are invisible to the congruence sieve, which is exactly why the
+  item needs the Λ/2Λ class syndrome for them (measured in the Python
+  instruments).
+
+No `sorry`, no `axiom`, no `native_decide`; compiled standalone under
+`leanprover/lean4:v4.28.0` against `LeechLeanGolay.olean` (the owner's
+`scripts/rebuild_lean.sh` has to confirm both in the full build).
+-/
